  <h1>Types of Traumatic Brain Injuries and Prognosis</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Greer Holiday (GH), Editor-in-Chief &mdash; Neurotrauma Practice. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Traumatic brain injury is not a single condition. It is a category covering a wide spectrum of injury patterns, mechanisms, severities, and outcomes. Understanding the specific type of injury and its expected recovery trajectory is essential for clinical management and for litigation valuation. This guide describes the major classification systems, the major injury types, and what the published literature says about prognosis.</p>

  <h2>Severity classification systems</h2>

  <p>Three frameworks dominate TBI severity classification in clinical and litigation contexts.</p>

  <h3>Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)</h3>

  <p>The GCS, developed at the University of Glasgow in 1974, is the universal acute-care severity score. It is scored 3&ndash;15 across three components:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eye opening:</strong> 1 (none) to 4 (spontaneous).</li>
    <li><strong>Verbal response:</strong> 1 (none) to 5 (oriented).</li>
    <li><strong>Motor response:</strong> 1 (none) to 6 (obeys commands).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The standard severity classifications:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>GCS 13&ndash;15:</strong> mild TBI / concussion.</li>
    <li><strong>GCS 9&ndash;12:</strong> moderate TBI.</li>
    <li><strong>GCS 3&ndash;8:</strong> severe TBI.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The lowest GCS documented in the first 24 hours is the conventional severity classification. Where a patient is intubated and sedated early, the GCS-T notation indicates the verbal component is untestable; the field GCS and pre-intubation hospital GCS should be located in the record.</p>

  <h3>Loss of consciousness (LOC) and post-traumatic amnesia (PTA)</h3>

  <p>Severity classification is often supplemented by LOC duration and PTA duration:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mild TBI:</strong> LOC less than 30 minutes (or no LOC), PTA less than 24 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Moderate TBI:</strong> LOC 30 minutes to 24 hours, PTA 1&ndash;7 days.</li>
    <li><strong>Severe TBI:</strong> LOC more than 24 hours, PTA more than 7 days.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>PTA is the period after injury during which the patient cannot form new memories. Duration of PTA is one of the better single predictors of long-term outcome, particularly for moderate TBI.</p>

  <h3>Rancho Los Amigos Cognitive Scale</h3>

  <p>The Rancho scale (developed at Rancho Los Amigos National Rehabilitation Center) describes cognitive recovery through eight (classic) or ten (revised) levels:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Level I:</strong> no response.</li>
    <li><strong>Level II:</strong> generalized response.</li>
    <li><strong>Level III:</strong> localized response.</li>
    <li><strong>Level IV:</strong> confused, agitated.</li>
    <li><strong>Level V:</strong> confused, inappropriate, non-agitated.</li>
    <li><strong>Level VI:</strong> confused, appropriate.</li>
    <li><strong>Level VII:</strong> automatic, appropriate.</li>
    <li><strong>Level VIII:</strong> purposeful, appropriate.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The Rancho scale is the workhorse of inpatient neuro-rehabilitation. The level at which a patient plateaus &mdash; or reaches at the time of life-care planning &mdash; is a strong predictor of long-term independence and care needs.</p>

  <h3>Disability Rating Scale (DRS)</h3>

  <p>The DRS combines arousal, cognitive function, dependency on others, and employability into a single score from 0 (no disability) to 29 (extreme vegetative state). DRS is widely used in TBI outcome research and is a useful summary number for damages presentation.</p>

  <h2>Focal vs diffuse injury</h2>

  <p>Beyond severity, TBI is classified by spatial pattern.</p>

  <h3>Focal injuries</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Contusion.</strong> Bruising of cortical surface, typically at the site of impact (coup) and on the opposite side (contrecoup). Frontal and temporal lobes are most often affected because they are pressed against bony skull prominences. Contusions are visible on CT and MRI. The functional deficits track the affected region: frontal contusion produces executive function deficits; temporal contusion produces memory deficits.</li>
    <li><strong>Subdural hematoma.</strong> Bleeding between the dura mater and the brain surface from torn bridging veins. Most common in the elderly (cerebral atrophy stretches the bridging veins) and in patients on anticoagulants. Acute subdural hematomas produce severe injury through mass effect and herniation. Chronic subdural hematomas can develop weeks after seemingly minor trauma and present with subtle cognitive decline.</li>
    <li><strong>Epidural hematoma.</strong> Bleeding between skull and dura, typically from a torn middle meningeal artery in the context of a temporal skull fracture. Classic clinical picture: brief LOC, lucid interval of minutes to hours, then rapid deterioration. Surgical evacuation can produce excellent outcomes if performed promptly; delayed diagnosis is catastrophic.</li>
    <li><strong>Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH).</strong> Blood in the subarachnoid space following trauma. Often present alongside other injury patterns. Traumatic SAH is distinct from spontaneous aneurysmal SAH but can present similarly on imaging.</li>
    <li><strong>Intracerebral hemorrhage.</strong> Bleeding within the brain parenchyma. Often associated with severe focal contusion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Diffuse injuries</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Diffuse axonal injury (DAI).</strong> Widespread stretching and shearing of axons throughout the brain, particularly at gray-white matter junctions, the corpus callosum, and the brainstem. Mechanism is rotational acceleration &mdash; high-speed motor vehicle accidents, falls from height, and shaken baby syndrome are classic causes. Standard CT often appears normal or shows only small petechial hemorrhages. MRI (susceptibility-weighted imaging) reveals the true injury extent. DAI is the major reason why some patients with normal CT have substantial cognitive impairment.</li>
    <li><strong>Hypoxic / anoxic brain injury.</strong> Diffuse injury from oxygen deprivation. Causes include cardiac arrest, near-drowning, strangulation, severe respiratory failure, and intrapartum hypoxia. The hippocampus and watershed cortical areas are most vulnerable. Anoxic injury commonly produces dense memory impairment (hippocampal damage) and (in severe cases) vegetative outcomes.</li>
    <li><strong>Diffuse cerebral swelling.</strong> Brain-wide edema following trauma. More common in pediatric TBI. Can produce severe injury through elevated intracranial pressure even without significant focal lesion.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Primary vs secondary injury</h2>

  <p>The clinical and forensic distinction between primary and secondary injury is critical.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary injury</strong> is the damage from the mechanical event itself. Fixed at the moment of trauma.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary injury</strong> is damage that develops over the following hours and days: ischemia from hypotension or hypoxia, mass effect from expanding hematoma, cerebral edema, herniation, seizure-related injury, and inflammatory cascades. Secondary injury is at least partly preventable with appropriate medical management. Preventable secondary injury is a common medical-malpractice theory in TBI cases.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Examples of preventable secondary injury: delayed recognition and evacuation of expanding epidural or subdural hematoma; failure to maintain adequate cerebral perfusion pressure in severe TBI; failure to manage intracranial pressure; failure to provide adequate seizure prophylaxis in high-risk patients; respiratory and circulatory failures during transport.</p>

  <h2>Recovery curves and prognosis</h2>

  <p>Recovery from TBI follows a generally predictable trajectory but with substantial individual variation.</p>

  <h3>Mild TBI recovery</h3>

  <p>Most mild TBI patients recover substantially within 4&ndash;12 weeks. However, a meaningful minority (estimated 15&ndash;30%, depending on definition and follow-up) develop persistent post-concussive symptoms. Risk factors for persistent symptoms include prior concussion history, female sex, older age, history of headache or migraine, history of mood or anxiety disorder, and severity of initial symptoms. Most cases of persistent post-concussive syndrome eventually improve but the time course can extend to years.</p>

  <h3>Moderate TBI recovery</h3>

  <p>Moderate TBI recovery is highly variable. The first 12&ndash;24 months show the most change; recovery beyond two years is less dramatic but continues. Vocational outcomes depend heavily on pre-injury occupation and the cognitive profile of the residual deficits. Many moderate TBI patients return to work, often with accommodations or in reduced-responsibility roles.</p>

  <h3>Severe TBI recovery</h3>

  <p>Severe TBI recovery is most variable. Some patients achieve substantial recovery; others remain dependent on supervision and assistance. The Rancho level at one year post-injury is a strong predictor of long-term independence. Patients who reach Rancho VIII have the best prognosis for independent living; those who plateau at Rancho IV&ndash;V often require ongoing supervision indefinitely.</p>

  <h3>Catastrophic outcomes</h3>

  <p>Vegetative state, minimally conscious state, and locked-in syndrome are catastrophic outcomes. Vegetative state involves preserved sleep-wake cycles without awareness; minimally conscious state involves intermittent purposeful behavior; locked-in syndrome (typically due to brainstem injury) involves preserved consciousness with profound motor paralysis. Lifetime 24-hour skilled care is required. Life expectancy is reduced but can extend decades, especially with good supportive care.</p>

  <h2>Post-concussive syndrome (PCS)</h2>

  <p>PCS describes persistent symptoms beyond the expected recovery window after mild TBI. Symptoms include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Headache (often migraine-pattern).</li>
    <li>Dizziness and vestibular dysfunction.</li>
    <li>Sleep disturbance.</li>
    <li>Cognitive difficulty (attention, concentration, memory, processing speed).</li>
    <li>Mood disturbance (irritability, anxiety, depression).</li>
    <li>Fatigue.</li>
    <li>Light and noise sensitivity.</li>
    <li>Oculomotor dysfunction (convergence insufficiency, accommodation difficulty).</li>
  </ul>

  <p>PCS is a real diagnosis with substantial peer-reviewed support. Documentation typically includes validated symptom inventories (RPQ, PCSS), neuropsychological testing showing measurable deficits, vestibular and oculomotor evaluation, and (where appropriate) MRI with sequences sensitive to diffuse injury.</p>

  <h2>Chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE)</h2>

  <p>CTE is a progressive neurodegenerative process associated with repeated head trauma. Currently diagnosable definitively only at autopsy through identification of characteristic tau protein deposition. Significant in contact sports (football, boxing, MMA, hockey, soccer at elite levels) and military service with repeated blast exposure. CTE is litigation-significant in repeated-trauma contexts, including the major NFL and NHL concussion settlements and ongoing cases involving youth and college football.</p>

  <h2>Second impact syndrome</h2>

  <p>Rare but catastrophic. A second concussion sustained before recovery from a first concussion produces diffuse cerebral swelling and severe outcome, with a high mortality rate. Most prominent in pediatric and adolescent athletes. Return-to-play protocols in organized sports are designed specifically to prevent second impact syndrome. Mismanagement of return-to-play is a litigation issue, particularly in school-sports contexts.</p>

  <h2>Pediatric TBI</h2>

  <p>Children are not small adults for TBI purposes. Pediatric TBI has:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Distinct mechanisms (shaken baby syndrome, falls from height appropriate to developmental stage).</li>
    <li>Distinct imaging considerations (open sutures, developing brain).</li>
    <li>Distinct prognosis patterns: very young brains have more plasticity but also more vulnerability to disrupted development.</li>
    <li>Distinct litigation considerations: longer life of damages, larger lost-earnings claims, conservatorship and special needs trust planning, longer statute of limitations in most states.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prognostic indicators</h2>

  <p>The following are the strongest predictors of long-term outcome:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Initial GCS (lowest in first 24 hours).</li>
    <li>Pupillary reactivity at presentation.</li>
    <li>Duration of PTA.</li>
    <li>Rancho level at three months.</li>
    <li>Age (younger generally better, with the exception of very young brains for some injury types).</li>
    <li>Presence and pattern of imaging findings (DAI extent on MRI, brainstem involvement).</li>
    <li>Secondary injury complications during acute course.</li>
  </ul>

